# M2 sandbox plan: prove adaptive research first

**Selected deployment:** one VM per lab and selective JiuwenBox reuse, with OpenJiuwen compatibility and upstream contribution potential as explicit targets. The complete AI4R, including RSI and factory, belongs to each lab. [[Designs/Sandbox Lab Design]] owns the selected placement and adapter boundaries; checkpoint 1 remains the comparison history. No deployment or compatibility result is implied.


The first delivery is one durable research lab that changes its next experiment when new evidence invalidates its approach. Use native JiuwenSwarm skills, tools/MCP, role visibility and creation/evolution hooks with existing AI4R checking and storage wherever they fit. Exercise one bounded factory-created or upgraded capsule as part of the lab slice, rather than adding a competing generic skill framework.

This replaces the earlier VM-first, carrier-first and factory-first delivery order. [[Designs/Sandbox Lab Design]] owns the current explanation and evidence; [[Designs/Labs and Capability Capsules]] is the short overview. The lab boundary and reuse direction are selected; detailed M2 integration remains a proposal. M1 runs and its published checkpoint retain their existing contracts.

## 1 Establish the smallest useful change

Agree one bounded research objective, authorized data, no-web/web policy, resource budget, independent success criteria and a discovery that should change the next step. Inspect the target repository's current instructions and native task allocation before implementation. Recheck the current source rather than treating a notebook snapshot as today's code.

The inspected M1 scheduler freezes one run and verifies same-run producer proofs. Its bounded planning continuation is not research adaptation. The pinned OpenJiuwen SDK already contains persistent goals and journal-based selective replay. Exercise those paths in a small compatibility spike: show what our binder/checking/store can reuse, what requires an adapter, and whether cache identity captures the dependencies needed for this case. A different runtime is justified only by a concrete gap and measured integration advantage.

**Exit:** a reviewable mapping of the existing goal, planner, scheduler, checks and store to one objective and two investigation batches; one selected native reuse path; explicit handling of accepted artifact transfer, multi-source CC intake and one factory candidate. No new generic registry, planning service, VM fleet or factory is required.

## 2 Demonstrate one evidence-driven revision

Implement persistent objective/knowledge/strategy state and a next-batch planning boundary. Begin after clarification and before substantive permitted investigation. Re-enter clarification when needed. Bind each dispatched batch through the existing protected path. Transfer accepted artifacts explicitly between batches with scope, dependency and check validation; do not relax current same-run proofs into arbitrary artifact reads.

Exercise a material late discovery: revise the dependent future experiment, retain unrelated valid findings, permit scoped local retry while independent work continues, and produce a supported next action or narrower conclusion. Keep user permissions authoritative. A candidate's recommendation or successful process exit cannot make its own result accepted or change the goal's completion criteria.

**Exit:** an actual Planner selects the changed next experiment after observing the result. One created or upgraded CC passes executable functional/task checks, independent qualification and native Skill/tool invocation. A specialist-vs-generalist improvement claim needs a matched comparison. Retained output and independent assessment show why the change was useful. A controlled driver establishes protocol behavior only. Include a no-web case, missing input, rejected output and a valid scientific negative. Stop at the cumulative cost/revision/no-progress limits with a visible outcome.

## 3 Make that lab survive interruption

Use one permitted Linux lab VM with its own Docker Engine, bounded jobs and a host/provider stop path independent of guest AI4R. Selectively reuse JiuwenBox behind an adapter while keeping OCI package execution and whole-VM control distinct. Keep project state and accepted artifacts outside replaceable app processes. Reconcile interrupted jobs and uncertain effects before retrying. Test restart and a verified restore into a replacement deployment, fencing the old writer. Exercise timeout/cancel, storage exhaustion and stale artifact rejection. Measurement interference must be controlled or reported.

**Exit:** the lab resumes the intended research state, avoids duplicate effects and preserves usable findings. Check effective access, network and resource limits on the actual host. A fixture-only success is not host isolation evidence. One VM lab and a small stateful fixture are sufficient; Spark, GPU and robotics remain optional profiles with separate qualification. Verify supported OpenJiuwen tool/provider behavior, actual cancellation/termination and retained-state restart at the selected pins. Preserve reproducible generic findings for possible upstream contributions.

## 4 Decide whether selective planning earns its cost

Compare fixed initial planning, revision at every batch boundary and selective revision. Keep capabilities, model, inputs, discovery schedule, permissions, total budget and evaluation criteria matched. Freeze the acceptable cost/quality tradeoff first. Score outcomes independently; include no-change tasks, scope changes, misleading observations, failed prerequisites and interrupted work. Repeat paired trials on held-out cases; report quality, stale reuse, recovery, planner/checking cost and elapsed time, including failures.

**Exit:** an evidence-based decision to keep, simplify or revise the mechanism. The smallest prototype proves feasibility; repeated controlled comparisons are needed for a value claim, and a representative extended run is needed for long-duration operation. The planner-strength/capability-composition study is separate so it does not confound this planning-policy comparison.

## Expand only where the evidence points

| Observed need | Next extension | Required evidence |
|---|---|---|
| Independent useful work is delayed by global barriers | Scoped patches while unaffected branches continue | Atomic revision conflict rejection, invalidation closure, cancellation/obsolete-output handling, no stale dispatch |
| Missing capability repeatedly blocks useful research | Native create/evolution hooks plus the factory packaging adapter | Measured integration effort, independent conformance and useful task contribution; compatible local future adoption |
| Stateful domain service is required | Managed/attached application binding | Session leases, reset/checkpoint behavior, recovery and compatible updates |
| A second project is needed | Second independent lab VM | Separate writable state/access, restore, selective contribution and measured resource cost |
| New harness behavior is useful | RSI-owned platform candidate | Evaluation against incumbent behavior; candidate cannot change its own acceptance criteria |

Factories still produce CC candidates, including packages with several operations. Qualification controls eligibility; automatic compatible local updates apply to new bindings only. Whole-harness RSI has its own owner. Central adoption is a separate decision. These responsibilities are retained without making every extension a prerequisite for testing adaptation.

## Decisions for our design walkthrough

Use the full deployment, internals, cycle, CC, inference and recovery diagrams to agree the first integration boundary. The presentation excludes a workload example. Agree how much quality improvement or wasted-work reduction would justify the extra cost. Review the goal/replay reuse spike and artifact-transfer rule before deciding whether live patching is necessary. Then show the objective, current evidence, next batch, revision reason and stop condition in the proposed presentation.

The honest presentation claim today is: our source has governed fixed-plan execution and upstream mechanisms worth reusing; this design proposes testing adaptive research on top. No improvement percentage, generalized day-long autonomy, cross-hardware predictor accuracy or deployed multi-lab factory is established.

Shared API inference may be co-located or remote. The bounded integration must also check per-lab limits, shared-service survival after lab stop, and denied cross-lab access to private model endpoints and checkpoints. See [[Designs/Sandbox Briefing/presentation/README]] for checkpoint 3 and the reference appendix.

Model access integration must inject routes into CC containers as well as AI4R. Validate aliases/features, reachable container networking, credential scoping and recorded model identity after endpoint relocation. A model change must follow explicit binding policy. See the model access contract in [[Designs/Sandbox Lab Design]].

Factory inputs include explicit user/AI requests, data/repos/papers, mental models/methods and repeated or critical gaps. General, domain and lab-owned package storage stay distinct. Qualified packages become local tools; upstream mother-AI4R adoption and generic JiuwenSwarm contributions follow separate review paths. [[Designs/Sandbox Lab Design]] owns this native integration contract.
